description:
Filed under api-contract,immutability because the fix is additive on a #[non_exhaustive] enum but the asymmetric contract is the immutability concern: once consumers ship handlers built around hand-rolled BackendSetError-to-JmapError conversions, every later attempt to add the defence-in-depth From impl risks behavioural change in their handlers.

Context: bd:JMAP-jfia.1 / bd:JMAP-wlip.2 closed the wire-leak in BackendChangesError via:

  impl<E: std::error::Error> From<BackendChangesError<E>> for jmap_types::JmapError {
      fn from(e: BackendChangesError<E>) -> Self {
          match e {
              BackendChangesError::Other(_inner) => {
                  jmap_types::JmapError::server_fail(SERVER_FAIL_INTERNAL_DESC)
              }
              ...
          }
      }
  }

and the matching ergonomic helper:

  impl<E> From<E> for BackendChangesError<E> { fn from(e: E) -> Self { Self::Other(e) } }

These two impls let consumers write:

  let result = backend.get_changes(...).await.map_err(JmapError::from)?;

which automatically routes through the redacted-Other arm without the consumer ever touching the inner error.

BackendSetError has NEITHER impl. Looking at crate-jmap-server/src/backend.rs:535-568, the only impl is From<SetError> for BackendSetError<E>. Consumers writing a handler that emits set errors must hand-roll the conversion at every call site:

  let result = backend.set_objects(...).await.map_err(|e| match e {
      BackendSetError::SetError(se) => /* embed as wire SetError */,
      BackendSetError::Other(inner) => /* THIS is where consumers leak */,
  })?;

The 'Other(inner)' arm is where a tired consumer writes:

  BackendSetError::Other(e) => JmapError::server_fail(e.to_string())

reintroducing exactly the wire leak that bd:JMAP-jfia.1 closed for BackendChangesError. The Display impl on BackendSetError already interpolates `{e}` for the inner — so a consumer that even calls `set_err.to_string()` to derive the wire description gets the full backend display string.

The class of risk:

1. Maintainer pressure: every consumer (jmap-mail-server, jmap-chat-server, jmap-calendars-server, ...) needs the same conversion. Each one will hand-roll it. Each hand-roll is an opportunity to leak.

2. Existing wire-leak defence is one-sided: bd:JMAP-jfia.1's redaction lives only on the changes path. The /set path — which is where credential-bearing fields (passwords, tokens, mailFrom addresses, attachment blobs) most often live in JMAP backends — has NO equivalent defence.

3. The SERVER_FAIL_INTERNAL_DESC constant and server_fail_from_backend helper exist for exactly this case. But they're a 'caller MUST remember to use them' contract, not a structural enforcement. Symmetric From impls on BackendChangesError were the structural enforcement; BackendSetError is missing the structural half.

Fix (additive, non-breaking):

  impl<E> From<E> for BackendSetError<E> {
      fn from(e: E) -> Self { Self::Other(e) }
  }

  impl<E: std::error::Error> From<BackendSetError<E>> for jmap_types::JmapError {
      fn from(e: BackendSetError<E>) -> Self {
          match e {
              BackendSetError::SetError(se) => {
                  // The SetError variant carries the SetError envelope intended
                  // for notCreated/notUpdated/notDestroyed maps, NOT a JmapError
                  // method-level error. Mapping it here is wrong shape — these
                  // are per-object set errors, not per-method errors. A From
                  // impl to JmapError can't preserve the right semantics.
                  // The correct shape is a method on BackendSetError that
                  // distinguishes set-error-vs-method-error.
                  ...
              }
              BackendSetError::Other(_inner) => {
                  jmap_types::JmapError::server_fail(SERVER_FAIL_INTERNAL_DESC)
              }
          }
      }
  }

Caveat above: the From-to-JmapError impl is harder than the changes case because SetError is per-object, not per-method. The right shape might be a method like `BackendSetError::into_jmap_error_or_set_error(self) -> Result<JmapError, SetError>` that callers consume.

Either way, the symmetric ergonomic surface MUST be filled in. Until it is, every consumer's hand-roll is an opportunity to leak — and the kit ships a structural guarantee for changes but not for set.

Workspace propagation: this lands once in jmap-server; every jmap-*-server extension picks up the new conversions transparently. No consumer breakage.

Priority: P1 because the security-defence class is the same as the closed bd:JMAP-jfia.1 and the surface is on every extension server.
